at you did, call these \"scheduled tasks\" (or whatever user is calling them) — never \"triggers\", \"routines\", or \"cron jobs\"; those are internal API names.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"at": {"description": "The fire time. For a clock time in a known IANA time zone (the user's '9am Monday'), write the local date and time, then the zone name in brackets, with no UTC offset (e.g. 2026-04-20T09:00:00[America/Los_Angeles]). The server works out the offset for that date, so do not work it out yourself. Otherwise write an RFC3339 timestamp (e.g. 2026-04-20T17:00:00Z). Seconds are truncated. Must be in the future. Mutually exclusive with 'delay_minutes' — set exactly one.", "type": "string"}, "delay_minutes": {"description": "Fire this many minutes from now. Minimum 1. Mutually exclusive with 'at' — set exactly one.", "minimum": 1, "type": "integer"}, "initiation": {"description": "Who wanted this message scheduled. Defaults to own_followup (your own check-in on in-flight work); pass human_request when a person asked you to remind them or to come back at a set time.", "enum": ["human_request", "human_schedule", "own_followup", "own_initiative"], "type": "string"}, "message": {"description": "The text to deliver. Write it assuming your current conversation context — this session continues, it does not start fresh.", "type": "string"}, "name": {"description": "Short human-readable label for this reminder as it appears in the user's list of scheduled tasks (e.g. \"Re-check PR #123 CI\"). A few words, one line. Optional — omit and one is derived from the message.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["message"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__send_later"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Send a message to another Claude Code Remote session. It arrives there as a turn marked as sent by this session, not typed by a person: the target is told to treat it as data from a peer, and not to change permission settings or take new destructive actions on it alone. The call returns when the message is delivered, not when it is answered; the target can reply with its own send_message, which arrives here as a new turn. Use this for meta-orchestration — e.g. asking a sibling session to perform a subtask.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__send_message", "schema": {"description": "Send a message to another Claude Code Remote session. It arrives there as a turn marked as sent by this session, not typed by a person: the target is told to treat it as data from a peer, and not to change permission settings or take new destructive actions on it alone. The call returns when the message is delivered, not when it is answered; the target can reply with its own send_message, which arrives here as a new turn. Use this for meta-orchestration — e.g. asking a sibling session to perform a subtask.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"attachments": {"description": "Optional. PROJECT CHANNEL SESSIONS only (a project's ambient session): uploads this session received that the target thread should read — each file_uuid is the file's id as your turn's uploads listing shows it (file_..., or a bare UUID) and must be on a person's message in this project. Refused from any other session or target.", "items": {"properties": {"file_uuid": {"type": "string"}, "path": {"type": "string"}}, "required": ["file_uuid"], "type": "object"}, "maxItems": 16, "type": "array"}, "in_reply_to": {"description": "STANDING sessions only, for visibility 'posted_to_shared_channel'. The ts of the message from your principal that this answers: copy it from that message's <standing_owner_message ts=\"...\"> envelope attribute (e.g. \"1700000000.000200\"). The server verifies it is one of your principal's own messages, then threads your reply under it. Omit it only when the message answers no specific message from your principal; the reply then threads under your principal's latest message. Invalid from any other session.", "type": "string"}, "message": {"description": "The message text. Bounded to 64 KiB.", "type": "string"}, "priority": {"description": "Optional queue-scheduling hint for the target session's event loop. One of: now, next, later. 'now' interrupts the current turn; 'next' joins the running turn without stopping it; 'later' waits for turn end. When omitted, the target session applies its default scheduling.", "enum": ["now", "next", "later"], "type": "string"}, "session_id": {"description": "The target session ID to send a message to. Required unless a STANDING session addresses by role via to — leave it empty then.", "type": "string"}, "slack_message_ts": {"description": "SLACK CHANNEL SESSIONS only, when messaging a thread session in your channel: the id of a person's <message> to hand over as their own words, ahead of your note. The server verifies it and delivers it verbatim, or fails with a reason. Send it only to a thread whose pending proposal it clearly answers, or to each thread the person named or their ask covers. A bare \"go\" typed in one thread goes to that thread only.", "type": "string"}, "thread_ts": {"description": "With to \"thread\": the Slack ts of the thread's root message (like \"1700000000.000200\").", "type": "string"}, "to": {"description": "STANDING sessions only: address the destination by role instead of session_id. \"parent\" is your channel session (the same destination as session_id \"@parent\"). \"thread\" is the dedicated session of a thread in your channel; pass thread_ts with it (your spawn context names your origin thread's ts when you have one). To answer your principal in a thread they asked in, combine to \"thread\" with visibility \"posted_to_shared_channel\" and in_reply_to. When a route would work, a refusal names it (usually to \"parent\"). Leave session_id empty when using to. The tool result states where the message was actually delivered. Invalid from any other session.", "enum": ["parent", "thread"], "type": "string"}, "visibility": {"description": "STANDING sessions only: where this message ends up. Invalid from any other session.", "enum": ["posted_to_shared_channel", "sent_to_shared_agent"], "type": "string"}}, "required": ["message"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__send_message"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Add and/or remove tags on existing sessions. Use for retroactively grouping related sessions under a label, or renaming a label (remove the old tag, add the new one) across multiple sessions at once.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__set_session_tags", "schema": {"description": "Add and/or remove tags on existing sessions. Use for retroactively grouping related sessions under a label, or renaming a label (remove the old tag, add the new one) across multiple sessions at once.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"add": {"description": "Tags to add. Duplicates are idempotent.", "items": {"type": "string"}, "type": "array"}, "remove": {"description": "Tags to remove. Missing tags are a no-op.", "items": {"type": "string"}, "type": "array"}, "session_ids": {"description": "Session IDs to retag.", "items": {"type": "string"}, "type": "array"}}, "required": ["session_ids"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__set_session_tags"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Rename an existing Claude Code Remote session. For tags use set_session_tags; lifecycle is not settable here — use archive_session to archive.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__set_session_title", "schema": {"description": "Rename an existing Claude Code Remote session. For tags use set_session_tags; lifecycle is not settable here — use archive_session to archive.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"session_id": {"description": "The target session ID.", "type": "string"}, "title": {"description": "New session title. Max 500 chars.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["session_id", "title"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__set_session_title"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Subscribe this session to GitHub activity on a pull request. Once subscribed, comments, reviews, CI results and other PR events are delivered into this conversation as <wake reason=\"external-event\"><event source=\"github\" ...> envelopes. This tool call is idempotent. Use this when asked to autofix, monitor, watch, or babysit a PR. A PR's events go to one session at a time. If the tool result says a Claude agent is already watching the PR, the call succeeded but this session will not receive events: another conversation's Claude session, or a Claude agent that labels the PRs it watches, holds them. This session can take over only once that one stops: it unsubscribes or ends, or its watching label is removed from the PR.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__subscribe_pr_activity", "schema": {"description": "Subscribe this session to GitHub activity on a pull request. Once subscribed, comments, reviews, CI results and other PR events are delivered into this conversation as <wake reason=\"external-event\"><event source=\"github\" ...> envelopes. This tool call is idempotent. Use this when asked to autofix, monitor, watch, or babysit a PR. A PR's events go to one session at a time. If the tool result says a Claude agent is already watching the PR, the call succeeded but this session will not receive events: another conversation's Claude session, or a Claude agent that labels the PRs it watches, holds them. This session can take over only once that one stops: it unsubscribes or ends, or its watching label is removed from the PR.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"owner": {"description": "The repository owner (user or organization name).", "type": "string"}, "pullNumber": {"description": "The pull request number.", "type": "integer"}, "repo": {"description": "The repository name.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["owner", "repo", "pullNumber"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__subscribe_pr_activity"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Move THIS session onto another existing cloud environment the user can already use, without replacing this machine. Only the session's network changes: requests through the session's HTTPS proxy follow the target environment's allowed hosts, normally within seconds. Files, running processes and this conversation stay as they are. The target's variables are NOT set now: they are set from the next restart of Claude on this machine. Its setup script does NOT run now or at a restart: it runs only if the machine is replaced. Calling this tool IS the request for permission: the user is shown an approval prompt with the target's name and network, and must approve in every permission mode. So do not ask the user in chat first. Use it to move to an environment that get_environment lists in `saved_for_session_repositories`, or when the user asked to move this session. First call get_environment with the target's environment_id, then copy its `switch_statement` unchanged into `switch_statement`: the call is refused, changing nothing, if it does not match the target as it is when the user approves. Environments with unrestricted network access or a wildcard host are refused: start a new session on those instead. Network secrets saved on the target are sent to their hosts once this session has moved, and those saved on the current environment stop being used, which can take up to an hour; the approval prompt lists the hosts of both. If the user declines, or the call is refused, do not ask again in chat; carry on in this environment and say what remains blocked.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__switch_environment", "schema": {"description": "Move THIS session onto another existing cloud environment the user can already use, without replacing this machine. Only the session's network changes: re